      "Explain the implication and the impact of globalization in situating the changing agrarian class structure in India.",
    modelAnswer:
      "Introduction: Economic globalisation -- trade liberalisation, integration into global agricultural markets, corporate agribusiness penetration, and technology/input market changes since 1991 -- has significantly restructured India's agrarian class relations beyond the older landlord-tenant-labourer framework.\n\nKey impacts: (i) Differentiation among the peasantry has intensified -- globalisation-linked market integration has benefited larger, capital-intensive, export-oriented farmers (contract farming for global agribusiness, cash-crop cultivation) while smallholder and marginal farmers face heightened price volatility, input-cost pressure (globalised seed/fertiliser/pesticide markets) and debt vulnerability, sharpening class differentiation within the peasantry itself (following the Marxist agrarian-question tradition, Utsa Patnaik's analysis of intensifying rural class polarisation). (ii) Rise of contract farming and corporate agribusiness -- global agri-corporations increasingly shape production decisions through contract arrangements, altering farmers' autonomy and creating new dependent relationships resembling a rural 'putting-out' system. (iii) Land-use shifts toward cash crops and export commodities, sometimes at the expense of food-crop security for subsistence-dependent smallholders. (iv) Agrarian distress and out-migration -- price volatility and debt stress linked to global market exposure have contributed to significant distress migration of marginal farmers and landless labourers to urban informal labour markets, changing the agrarian class structure's composition by shrinking the resident agricultural labour force in some regions. (v) Technology-driven changes (mechanisation, in some cases enabled by global capital/equipment markets) have reduced demand for agricultural labour, affecting the position of landless labourers.\n\nCritical assessment: while some capital-rich farmers have benefited from globalisation's market access and technology diffusion, the dominant sociological finding (Patnaik, various agrarian-distress studies) is that globalisation has intensified inequality within the agrarian class structure -- widening the gap between capitalised farmers integrated into global value chains and marginal/landless cultivators facing heightened precarity, debt and displacement pressure, a pattern visible in the recurring farmer-distress and farmers'-protest movements of the past two decades.\n\nConclusion: globalisation has not simply modernised Indian agriculture uniformly, but has restructured agrarian class relations along lines of differential access to capital, markets and technology, generally sharpening rather than reducing class polarisation within the peasantry.",
