    questionText: "Citing some case studies, expand the concept of 'Development-induced Displacement'.",
    modelAnswer:
      "Development-induced displacement (DID) refers to the forced relocation of communities -- typically involuntary and disproportionately affecting the poor, tribal and rural populations -- caused by large-scale development projects such as dams, mines, industrial corridors, and infrastructure/urban expansion projects.\n\nKey features: displaced populations frequently face inadequate compensation, loss of land-based livelihoods without comparable alternatives, cultural and community disruption (especially severe for tribal groups with land-based identity and kinship structures), and prolonged, often unresolved, rehabilitation processes.\n\nCase studies: (i) The Narmada Bachao Andolan (Sardar Sarovar Dam, Narmada Valley) -- one of India's most studied DID cases, displacing tens of thousands, predominantly tribal and rural households, sparking a sustained social movement over inadequate rehabilitation (led by Medha Patkar). (ii) The Tehri Dam (Uttarakhand) -- large-scale displacement with contested resettlement outcomes. (iii) POSCO and Vedanta/Niyamgiri mining projects in Odisha -- displacement and land-alienation conflicts involving tribal communities (Dongria Kondh), where Supreme Court intervention (the Niyamgiri gram sabha referendum) became a landmark case affirming tribal consent rights under the Forest Rights Act. (iv) Coal-mining displacement in Jharkhand and Chhattisgarh, disproportionately affecting Adivasi populations.\n\nThese cases collectively illustrate that DID in India disproportionately burdens tribal and marginalised rural populations, and that rehabilitation and resettlement frameworks (despite the 2013 Land Acquisition Act's stronger provisions) remain frequently inadequate relative to the scale of displacement caused.",
