    modelAnswer:
      "The National Education Policy (NEP) 2020 replaced India's 1986 policy framework, proposing wide-ranging structural and pedagogical reforms across school and higher education.\n\nSalient features: (i) Restructured school system -- replacing the 10+2 structure with a 5+3+3+4 framework aligned to cognitive developmental stages (foundational, preparatory, middle, secondary), with stronger emphasis on early childhood care and education (ages 3-6). (ii) Multilingual and mother-tongue emphasis -- promoting instruction in mother tongue/regional language at least through grade 5 where possible, alongside a flexible three-language formula. (iii) Reduced curriculum content in favour of critical thinking, conceptual understanding and experiential/holistic learning over rote memorisation. (iv) Vocational education integration from grade 6 onward, aiming to reduce the academic-vocational divide. (v) Higher education reforms -- multiple entry-exit options with an Academic Bank of Credits, a four-year undergraduate degree with research option, dissolution of rigid discipline silos in favour of multidisciplinary education, and the establishment of a single regulatory body (Higher Education Commission of India) replacing multiple existing regulators. (vi) Target of 6% of GDP public investment in education and universalising access to school education by 2030. (vii) Technology integration (National Educational Technology Forum) for digital learning.\n\nCritics note implementation challenges around resourcing, teacher training, and the practicality of mother-tongue instruction across India's linguistically diverse and often under-resourced school systems.",
