    questionText: "Discuss the nature of regional variations in sex ratio in India. Stating reasons thereof.",
    modelAnswer:
      "India's sex ratio (females per 1000 males) exhibits significant regional variation, revealing deep-seated socio-cultural rather than purely biological determinants.\n\nRegional pattern: northern and northwestern states (Haryana, Punjab, parts of Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh) have historically recorded markedly adverse (low female-to-male) sex ratios and especially skewed child sex ratios, while southern states (Kerala, Tamil Nadu) and several northeastern states have recorded comparatively favourable, sometimes female-majority, sex ratios.\n\nReasons for the variation: (i) Son preference is culturally stronger in northern patrilineal, patrilocal kinship systems where sons are seen as essential for lineage continuity, old-age support, and property inheritance, combined with dowry-linked perception of daughters as economic burdens -- driving historically higher female infanticide/neglect and, since the 1980s-90s, sex-selective abortion enabled by ultrasound technology (particularly severe in Haryana and Punjab despite their relative economic prosperity, showing prosperity alone does not correct the imbalance). (ii) Southern states' generally more favourable female sex ratios are linked to historically better female literacy, workforce participation, comparatively lower dowry burden in some communities, and in Kerala's case, matrilineal inheritance traditions (Nair community) and strong social-development indicators (Kerala model of development). (iii) Legal measures (PCPNDT Act banning sex-selective testing, Beti Bachao Beti Padhao scheme) have had uneven regional impact, given continued enforcement gaps.\n\nThis regional pattern demonstrates that sex ratio imbalance is driven primarily by patriarchal kinship structure and son preference, not poverty alone.",
