    questionText:
      "How do you account for the increasing significance of religion in public and personal spheres in the context of secularization thesis in India? Explain.",
    modelAnswer:
      "Introduction: Classical secularisation theory (Weber's disenchantment, later systematised by Peter Berger and Bryan Wilson) predicted that modernisation -- industrialisation, education, science, rationalisation -- would progressively diminish religion's social significance in both public institutions and personal life. India's contemporary trajectory, however, shows religion's INCREASING rather than declining visibility and significance, requiring explanation.\n\nEvidence of increasing religious significance: growing religiosity in personal life (rising participation in religious tourism, festivals, and ritual observance even among educated, urban, professionally successful Indians); the political mainstreaming of religious-nationalist discourse and religiously inflected political mobilisation; increased visibility of religious identity in public life (religious symbols, personal-law debates, communal politics); and the flourishing of new/reformed religious movements and gurus with large urban, educated followings.\n\nAccounting for this trend, against the classical secularisation thesis: (i) T.N. Madan and Ashis Nandy's critique of secularisation theory as a Western, ethnocentric model that misreads non-Western societies -- they argue religion in India was never a purely 'private' matter separable from public/political life the way European secularisation theory assumed, making its 'resurgence' partly an artefact of a flawed initial theoretical expectation rather than a genuine reversal. (ii) Post-liberalisation identity anxiety -- rapid economic change, urban dislocation and status uncertainty under globalisation have, per several sociologists, driven a search for stable religious/communal identity anchors as a psychological and social response to disorienting modernisation (paralleling global patterns of religious resurgence amid globalisation-linked anxiety). (iii) Political instrumentalisation -- religious identity has been actively mobilised by political actors for electoral consolidation, deliberately increasing religion's public salience rather than this being a spontaneous cultural process alone. (iv) Modern technology (satellite TV, social media) has actually EXPANDED religion's reach and visibility (televangelist gurus, viral religious content), showing modernisation's tools can amplify rather than erode religious significance -- a direct empirical challenge to simple secularisation-equals-modernisation logic. (v) Peter Berger himself later revised his own secularisation thesis, acknowledging global religious resurgence as a major, unanticipated late-20th/21st-century pattern (his 'desecularization of the world' reassessment).\n\nConclusion: India's experience -- alongside Berger's own revision -- suggests classical secularisation theory's linear modernisation-erodes-religion model does not hold universally; religion's persistence and rising public salience in India reflects both a flawed Western-derived initial theory (Madan/Nandy) and genuine contemporary drivers (identity anxiety, political instrumentalisation, media amplification) that actively increase rather than diminish religion's social significance.",
    examinerNotes:
      "T.N. Madan and Ashis Nandy's critique of secularisation theory as ethnocentric, and Peter Berger's own later revision ('desecularization of the world'), are the specific theoretical citations that distinguish a strong answer from a purely descriptive account of rising religiosity.",
    sourceNote: SOURCE_B("6a"),
