    questionText: "What actionable measures would you suggest to curb the recurrent child labour menace in India?",
    modelAnswer:
      "Despite constitutional (Article 24) and legal prohibitions (Child Labour Prohibition and Regulation Act, amended 2016), child labour persists in India, particularly in agriculture, home-based industries, domestic work and informal manufacturing, rooted in poverty, weak enforcement and social acceptance in certain sectors.\n\nActionable measures: (i) Strengthen enforcement -- increase labour inspection capacity, impose stricter penalties on employers, and close loopholes permitting 'family enterprise' child labour that is frequently exploited to disguise commercial employment. (ii) Address root economic causes -- expand direct income support and employment guarantee schemes (strengthening MGNREGA-style safety nets) for poor households, since child labour is substantially driven by family economic distress. (iii) Universalise access to quality, genuinely free schooling with adequate mid-day meal and scholarship incentives, making schooling a more viable and attractive alternative to child labour -- linking Right to Education Act implementation more effectively to at-risk households. (iv) Supply-chain accountability -- mandate corporate due diligence and certification (e.g. in carpet, garment, and gem-cutting export industries historically linked to child labour) to eliminate child labour from formal and informal supply chains. (v) Community-level awareness and NGO partnership (building on models like the Kailash Satyarthi-founded Bachpan Bachao Andolan) to identify, rescue and rehabilitate child labourers with follow-up education/vocational support, not just removal. (vi) Strengthen birth registration and identity systems to make age verification and enforcement more effective.",
