      "Do you think that the decades of Dalit political mobilizations and movements have helped in strengthening India's democracy? Substantiate your arguments with facts.",
    modelAnswer:
      "Introduction: Dalit political mobilisation -- from the Ambedkarite movement through post-independence electoral and social-movement organising -- represents one of independent India's most significant instances of a historically excluded group actively claiming democratic citizenship, with substantial (though incomplete) effects on strengthening Indian democracy.\n\nHow Dalit mobilisation has strengthened democracy: (i) Constitutional and legal gains -- Ambedkar's leadership in drafting the Constitution embedded anti-untouchability provisions (Article 17), reservations in education/employment/legislatures (Articles 15(4), 16(4), 330, 332), and later the SC/ST Prevention of Atrocities Act (1989), institutionalising formal democratic protections that would not have existed without sustained Dalit political pressure. (ii) Electoral mobilisation and representation -- the rise of Dalit-led political formations (the Republican Party of India, and most significantly the Bahujan Samaj Party under Kanshi Ram and Mayawati) transformed Dalits from a passive vote bank into an organised, assertive electoral bloc, producing India's first Dalit-led state government (Uttar Pradesh) and demonstrating democracy's genuine capacity for historically marginalised groups to capture political power through the ballot. (iii) Deepening substantive democracy -- Dalit movements have pushed democratic practice beyond mere procedural voting rights toward genuine social and dignity-based citizenship, challenging caste-based social exclusion in public institutions, temples, and civic spaces, and building grassroots political consciousness and participation (panchayat-level reservation for SCs under the 73rd Amendment). (iv) Cultural and ideological contribution -- Ambedkarite thought has provided India's most systematic indigenous critique of social hierarchy and articulation of substantive equality, enriching Indian democratic and constitutional discourse itself.\n\nLimitations and continuing challenges: despite these gains, Dalit political power has not eliminated social discrimination, atrocity incidents (documented in NCRB data) persist, and some argue electoral mobilisation (particularly identity-based coalition politics) has at times prioritised elite Dalit leadership interests over grassroots welfare, and continuing economic marginalisation of large sections of the Dalit population shows political mobilisation alone has not achieved full substantive equality.\n\nConclusion: Dalit political mobilisation has substantially and measurably strengthened Indian democracy by institutionalising legal protections, achieving real electoral representation and power, and deepening democratic practice beyond formal proceduralism -- even though significant gaps between formal political gains and substantive social/economic equality remain.",
