    modelAnswer:
      "The rural-urban continuum concept challenges the classical rural-urban dichotomy (associated with Tonnies' Gemeinschaft/Gesellschaft and Redfield's folk-urban continuum), arguing that rural and urban are not sharply bounded, opposite categories but exist along a graduated spectrum of intermediate social forms sharing overlapping features.\n\nExplanation: rather than a binary break, real settlements exhibit a continuum -- from purely agrarian villages, through peri-urban/rurban settlements (villages absorbed into expanding metropolitan peripheries, exhibiting mixed agricultural and urban-employment characteristics), to small towns and finally large metropolitan cities. Migration, commuting, media penetration, market integration and non-farm employment diversification blur the traditional rural-urban social and economic boundary.\n\nIndian examples: villages on the outskirts of expanding cities (Delhi NCR's peri-urban villages, Bengaluru's urban-fringe villages) show rapid land-use conversion, non-agricultural employment, and lifestyle changes while retaining some rural social structures (caste panchayats, agricultural land ownership); large 'census towns' (rural areas administratively reclassified as urban due to non-agricultural employment share, per Census criteria) exemplify this continuum concretely; and circular labour migration (workers commuting or cyclically migrating between village and city) sustains ongoing rural-urban social and economic linkage rather than a clean rural-to-urban transition.\n\nSignificance: the continuum concept better captures contemporary India's blurred settlement reality than a rigid rural/urban binary.",
