    questionText: "Explain the thematic linkages between 'Patriarchy' and 'Honour killing' in India, citing some recent cases.",
    modelAnswer:
      "Introduction: Honour killing -- the murder of a family member (overwhelmingly women, and men who partner with them) for perceived transgression of family/community 'honour' norms, typically involving marriage or relationship choices -- is not an isolated criminal aberration but a direct, extreme manifestation of underlying patriarchal control over female sexuality, choice and family reputation.\n\nThematic linkages: (i) Control over female autonomy -- patriarchal ideology treats women's marital and sexual choices as family/community property rather than individual rights; honour killings occur precisely when women exercise autonomous choice (inter-caste, inter-religious, or self-arranged 'love' marriages) that patriarchal family structures perceive as a loss of control and a threat to family reputation. (ii) Caste and community endogamy enforcement -- patriarchy in India is deeply intertwined with caste hierarchy; honour killings disproportionately occur in cases of inter-caste marriage, especially where a woman from a higher/dominant caste marries a man from a lower caste, since this is seen as doubly transgressive -- violating both gender and caste hierarchy simultaneously. (iii) Khap panchayats and extra-legal community sanctioning -- informal caste/community councils, particularly in parts of Haryana, western UP and Rajasthan, have historically sanctioned or pressured families toward honour killings, reflecting institutionalised patriarchal-caste authority operating alongside, and sometimes in defiance of, the formal legal system. (iv) 'Honour' as a patriarchal social currency -- family/community status is measured partly through control over women's conduct, making violence against transgressing women a means of publicly restoring perceived lost honour and reasserting patriarchal authority before the community.\n\nRecent illustrative cases: continuing incidents in Haryana, Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan and Tamil Nadu of inter-caste couples murdered by family members (several documented and prosecuted under IPC provisions and, following Shakti Vahini v. Union of India, 2018, under Supreme Court directives mandating state action against khap-sanctioned violence and protection for consenting adult couples) illustrate the continuing prevalence of the phenomenon despite legal prohibition.\n\nPolicy/legal response: the Supreme Court's Shakti Vahini judgment directed states to set up special cells and safe houses for threatened couples and treat khap panchawat interference as illegal, though enforcement remains inconsistent given entrenched local social sanction for such violence.\n\nConclusion: honour killing is best understood not as isolated familial violence but as patriarchy's most extreme enforcement mechanism, intersecting directly with caste endogamy norms, making it a specifically Indian articulation of gender-based violence rooted in the intersection of patriarchal and caste ideologies.",
