      "Discuss the challenges faced by the cooperative movements in India. Suggest measures to strengthen the movement at the grass-roots level.",
    modelAnswer:
      "Introduction: India's cooperative movement -- spanning credit cooperatives, agricultural marketing cooperatives (notably dairy cooperatives like Amul/Anand pattern), and consumer cooperatives -- has historically been envisioned as a democratic, self-help alternative to both purely state-run and purely private-capitalist economic organisation, but faces persistent structural challenges.\n\nChallenges: (i) Excessive government control and bureaucratisation -- many cooperatives, particularly credit and agricultural marketing cooperatives, have become heavily dependent on and controlled by state government departments and political patronage rather than functioning as genuinely member-driven democratic institutions, undermining the cooperative principle of self-governance. (ii) Political interference and elite capture -- cooperative leadership positions are frequently captured by locally dominant, politically connected elites (often from dominant landholding castes), diverting benefits away from smaller/marginal members and undermining democratic participation. (iii) Financial mismanagement and weak professional capacity -- many cooperative credit institutions suffer from poor loan recovery, financial irregularities and inadequate professional management expertise. (iv) Limited member participation and awareness -- ordinary members, particularly small and marginal farmers, often have low awareness of their rights and limited actual participation in cooperative governance and decision-making. (v) Competition from private and corporate players -- liberalisation has exposed cooperatives (particularly in dairy, sugar and marketing) to more efficient and better-capitalised private-sector competition. (vi) Regional unevenness -- successful models (Amul in Gujarat, Kerala's cooperative sector) coexist with widespread dysfunction and financial distress in cooperative institutions in many other states.\n\nMeasures to strengthen at the grassroots level: (i) Reduce bureaucratic and political control, granting cooperatives genuine functional and financial autonomy consistent with the 97th Constitutional Amendment's cooperative-autonomy provisions. (ii) Strengthen member education and capacity-building programmes to increase informed grassroots participation in governance and decision-making. (iii) Introduce professional management and financial audit standards while preserving democratic member-control structures (professionalisation without de-democratisation). (iv) Replicate successful federated structures like the Amul three-tier model (village-district-state cooperative federation) in other sectors and regions, adapting its incentive-aligned, farmer-first design. (v) Expand digital technology use for transparent record-keeping, transaction tracking and member communication, reducing opportunities for elite capture and mismanagement. (vi) Strengthen women's and marginal farmers' representation within cooperative governance structures to broaden genuine grassroots participation.\n\nConclusion: cooperative movements retain real potential as a democratic, equity-oriented economic model, but require depoliticisation, professionalisation and genuine grassroots capacity-building to overcome persistent elite capture and bureaucratic control.",
