    questionText: "What is 'Ageing'? Discuss the major problems of aged people in India.",
    modelAnswer:
      "Ageing refers to the progressive biological, psychological and social process of growing older, and at the population level, to the demographic phenomenon of a rising proportion of elderly persons (conventionally 60+ in India) within a society -- a trend accelerating in India due to declining fertility and rising life expectancy.\n\nMajor problems faced by aged people in India: (i) Economic insecurity -- the vast majority of India's elderly, particularly in the informal sector, lack pension coverage or adequate retirement savings, leaving many dependent on family support or continued precarious work. (ii) Health challenges -- rising incidence of chronic and age-related illnesses, combined with inadequate geriatric healthcare infrastructure and high out-of-pocket medical costs. (iii) Weakening of traditional family-based old-age support -- nuclearisation of families, migration of adult children for employment, and changing intergenerational living arrangements have eroded the traditional joint-family safety net elderly Indians historically relied upon, increasingly leaving elderly parents alone or in old-age homes. (iv) Social isolation and loneliness, particularly for widowed or single elderly persons living apart from family. (v) Elder abuse and neglect, including financial exploitation, documented by studies like HelpAge India's surveys. (vi) Gender dimension -- elderly women face compounded vulnerability through widowhood, lower lifetime asset ownership, and weaker independent economic means compared to elderly men.\n\nPolicy responses (Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act, 2007; National Policy on Older Persons) exist but face implementation gaps.",
